===== Known Issues =====

  * WiFi on LEDE 17.01.x and older OpenWrt releases: 
      * WiFi is supported by the [[https://dev.openwrt.org/browser/trunk/package/kernel/mt76/|mt76]] driver and initial testing shows that WiFi is stable. But WiFi has some problems with older versions < =17.01.2. 
      * WiFi is especially slower compared to vendor firmware on version < =17.01.2 (This actually affects all devices with the MediaTek MT7612E chip). WiFi performance is noticably improved on later snapshots > ~2017-07-20 and in general with OpenWrt releases >17.01. 
      * WiFi on 2.4 GHz is known to have major issues, when USB3.0 hardware with bad shielding gets attached to the device. 
      * WiFi range of the internal antennas is ok, but can also be improved by adding external antennas.
  * LEDs on LEDE < = 17.01.2 and older OpenWrt releases: The LEDs will not show any operation status on the device LEDs.

===== Recovery Mode =====
Like several other D-Link devices, the bootloader of this device contains an additional tiny emergency loader that is able to boot a web GUI that allows to reflash the firmware in case the regular flash procedure has failed and in case the firmware is broken. No serial cable is needed for this recovery mode.

Screenshot of this built-in recovery mode function, called "Emergency Room" (This is a D-Link provided boot loader function that will remain available, even after OpenWrt has been flashed to the device):\\
{{media:dlink:dlink_emergency_room_recovery_mode.png?0x150}}

Even if the device won't boot anymore due to a failed flash attempt, it is still possible, to trigger this emergency web GUI with the following boot procedure:
  * Push and hold reset button (on the bottom of the device) until power led starts flashing (about 10 secs or so) while plugging in the power cable. 
  * Give it ~30 seconds, to boot the recovery mode GUI
  * Connect your client computer to LAN1 of the device
  * Set your client IP address manually to 192.168.0.2 / 255.255.255.0.
  * Call the recovery page for the device at http://192.168.0.1
  * Use the provided emergency web GUI to upload and flash a new firmware to the device


You can interact with the recovery mode on Linux without needing a browser; just execute the following command from where your OpenWrt firmware binary is located, and wait until the router has flashed and rebooted.

    curl -v -i -F "firmware=@openwrt-ramips-mt7621-dlink_dir-860l-b1-squashfs-factory.bin" 192.168.0.1

Notes:
  * If you want to take a look first, the recovery mode can be safely accessed without resetting the active firmware or any active configuration, as long as you do not start the flash procedure.
  * You can also use the recovery mode as a primary option, to flash new OpenWrt releases or to return to a vendor provided stock firmware version.
  * If you use the recovery mode to apply followup OpenWrt updates to the device, always pick a factory.bin file, not a sysupgrade.bin file (Flashing a OpenWrt sysupgrade.bin file with the recovery mode will not permanently damage the device, but the device will not boot afterwards except for another recovery mode attempt).


